= Commons =

== Overview ==
'''Commons''' is a shared resource or resource system governed, used, or accessed by a community or multiple participants under some set of formal or informal rules.

Many classic commons problems concern common-pool resources: resources from which exclusion is difficult while one participant's extraction subtracts from what remains for others.

A commons is not defined by inevitable collapse. Empirical institutional research shows that communities can sometimes govern shared resources sustainably through boundaries, monitoring, graduated sanctions, conflict-resolution processes, nested institutions, and locally adapted rules.

Digital and knowledge commons may differ because information can be largely non-rival even when infrastructure, attention, moderation, or funding remain scarce.

== Boundaries ==
* commons ≠ public good
* commons ≠ unowned resource by definition
* commons ≠ inevitable tragedy

== Related concepts ==
[[Collective action]], [[Public good]], [[Governance]], [[Incentive]], [[Reputation system]], and [[Network]] provide the institutional context.

== Information commons as a coordination system ==
An '''information commons''' involves shared access to or stewardship of information resources, with rules governing contributions, use, maintenance, correction or reuse. In an open knowledge system, governance can determine who may submit edits, how revisions are reviewed, how provenance is retained and how conflicts are resolved. These are inspectable workflow and coordination mechanisms, not proof that commons universally self-organize.

A digital commons can have information that is cheaply reproducible while depending on rival inputs such as computing infrastructure, moderation time, funding or scarce expert attention. The resource, access rights and maintenance duties therefore need separate description. A community-maintained dataset is not identical to an unowned natural resource.

The computational interest lies in versioned shared knowledge, distributed collaboration, [[Related to::Governance|governance]] rules and incentive-compatible contribution workflows. Evidence about traditional common-pool resource governance offers comparisons, not automatic results for online communities or AI-agent swarms.

